-- Version 4 → 5: every project gains a "Ready for test" column, before Closed.
--
-- The first migration here that changes no schema at all. Nothing is created,
-- altered or dropped — `statuses` already stores board columns as rows, one per
-- project, so a new default column is data. That makes MigrationTest blind to
-- this file by construction: it compares PRAGMA output, and PRAGMA output is
-- identical either side of an INSERT. `5.db` is a byte-for-byte-equivalent
-- shape to `4.db` and exists only because the version moved. The row this file
-- inserts is covered by StatusMigrationTest instead.
--
-- The create path does not run this. A fresh volume gets its columns from
-- ProjectRepository's DEFAULT_STATUSES at the moment each project is created —
-- see that list, which now carries the same name. This file is the other half:
-- the projects on the deployed volume that already exist and would otherwise
-- keep their five columns forever.
--
-- ── Why this anchors on requires_resolution and not on the name ─────────────
--
-- "Before Closed" is the requirement, and `name = 'Closed'` is the obvious way
-- to write it and the wrong one, for the reason Statuses.sq gives at length: the
-- seed names that column but a project may rename it, and this volume has been
-- serving long enough for that to have happened. A migration keyed on the name
-- would silently do nothing for a renamed project — no error, no row, and a
-- board that quietly disagrees with every other board.
--
-- So the anchor is the flag, which is the durable fact: the closing column is
-- the one that demands a resolution, whatever it is called. MIN() because
-- Statuses.sq allows a project to flag a second column, and the leftmost one is
-- the one "before Closed" means.
--
-- ── The COALESCE tail is the project that has no flagged column ─────────────
--
-- Possible today: the settings dialog can turn `requires_resolution` off, and
-- nothing stops an admin turning it off everywhere. Such a project has no
-- "before Closed" to insert before, so the new column goes on the end, which is
-- the only answer that is not a guess. The second tail — position 0 — is for a
-- project with no statuses at all. That should be unreachable, because seeding
-- is one transaction with the project insert, but `position` is NOT NULL and an
-- unreachable NULL here would be a failed migration on startup rather than a
-- bad row, so it is worth the four characters.
--
-- ── Why both statements skip a project that already has the name ────────────
--
-- `UNIQUE (project_id, name)` on a COLLATE NOCASE column. A project whose admin
-- already made a "Ready for test" column by hand — or a "ready for test" one —
-- would violate it, and a violation here is not a bad row: it aborts the
-- migration, which aborts startup, on the deployed volume, for everyone. The
-- guard is on the shift as well as the insert so the two cannot disagree and
-- leave a project shifted but not filled.

-- Make room. Positions have no UNIQUE constraint (see Statuses.sq's setPosition
-- for why), so this does not need to walk the columns in any particular order.
UPDATE statuses
SET position = position + 1
WHERE position >= (
        SELECT MIN(anchor.position)
        FROM statuses anchor
        WHERE anchor.project_id = statuses.project_id
          AND anchor.requires_resolution = 1
    )
  AND NOT EXISTS (
        SELECT 1 FROM statuses existing
        WHERE existing.project_id = statuses.project_id
          AND existing.name = 'Ready for test'
    );

-- Fill the gap the shift just opened. MIN(position) - 1 rather than the value
-- the statement above compared against, because that value has itself moved:
-- the flagged column is now one to the right, so the free slot is one to its
-- left. Reading it back after the fact is what makes this correct for a project
-- whose closing column sat at position 0 — the shift moved it to 1, and this
-- lands on 0 rather than on -1.
--
-- requires_resolution is 0: Closed stays the one column that demands a
-- resolution. "Ready for test" is work handed over, not work finished.
INSERT INTO statuses (project_id, name, position, requires_resolution)
SELECT
    p.id,
    'Ready for test',
    COALESCE(
        (SELECT MIN(a.position) - 1 FROM statuses a
         WHERE a.project_id = p.id AND a.requires_resolution = 1),
        (SELECT MAX(m.position) + 1 FROM statuses m WHERE m.project_id = p.id),
        0
    ),
    0
FROM projects p
WHERE NOT EXISTS (
    SELECT 1 FROM statuses e
    WHERE e.project_id = p.id AND e.name = 'Ready for test'
);
